---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { appStoreUrl, APP_IDS } from '../../lib/seo';
import { GUIDES } from '../../lib/guides';

/**
 * HamCram guide: a two-week Technician study plan.
 * Exam facts (35 questions, pass at 26, one question per group, 10
 * subelements) are the published NCVEC / FCC Part 97 structure. The
 * per-subelement split below is the shape recent pools have used; the
 * copy tells readers to confirm against the pool they are studying.
 * Do not promise a pass. Do not claim HamCram features the product page
 * does not list.
 */
const guide = GUIDES.find((g) => g.path === '/guides/technician-exam-two-weeks')!;
const storeUrl = appStoreUrl(APP_IDS.hamCram, 'web-article-technician-two-weeks');

const subelements = [
  ['T1', 'FCC rules, station license responsibilities', '6'],
  ['T2', 'Operating procedures', '3'],
  ['T3', 'Radio wave characteristics and propagation', '3'],
  ['T4', 'Amateur radio practices and station setup', '2'],
  ['T5', 'Electrical principles', '4'],
  ['T6', 'Electrical components and circuits', '4'],
  ['T7', 'Station equipment', '4'],
  ['T8', 'Modulation modes and operating activities', '4'],
  ['T9', 'Antennas and feed lines', '2'],
  ['T0', 'Safety', '3'],
];

const plan = [
  ['Day 1', 'Take a cold 35-question practice exam before studying anything. The score does not matter; the point is to see the shape of the test and to find out which subelements already make sense to you.'],
  ['Day 2', 'T1, FCC rules. Read every question in the subelement once with its correct answer, then flashcards until you can call the answer before flipping. Finish with a 25-question quiz limited to T1.'],
  ['Day 3', 'T1 again, quickly, then T2 operating procedures. Rules and procedures are the memorization block; get them done early so they have time to settle.'],
  ['Day 4', 'T5 electrical principles. Learn Ohm’s law and the power formula well enough to rearrange them, then drill the arithmetic questions until they are boring.'],
  ['Day 5', 'T6 components and T7 station equipment. Mostly vocabulary: what a part is called, what it does, which block of a radio handles what.'],
  ['Day 6', 'T3 propagation and T9 antennas. Understand why VHF goes line of sight, why a longer wavelength means a lower frequency, and how antenna length tracks frequency.'],
  ['Day 7', 'Rest day for new material. One 50-question mixed quiz, then review only what you missed.'],
  ['Day 8', 'T8 modulation modes and operating activities: FM, SSB, digital modes, repeaters, satellites. Vocabulary plus a few numbers.'],
  ['Day 9', 'T4 station setup and T0 safety. Safety is the most common-sense subelement; the RF exposure and electrical questions still have specific answers to remember.'],
  ['Day 10', 'Full 35-question practice exam under exam conditions: no notes, no pausing. Then go through every miss and read the question with its correct answer twice.'],
  ['Day 11', 'Weak spots only. Whatever subelement your mastery is lowest in, that is the whole day.'],
  ['Day 12', 'Two full practice exams, morning and evening. If both clear 30 or better, you are ready. If not, the misses tell you what Day 13 is for.'],
  ['Day 13', 'Light review of the misses from Day 12. Handle the logistics: FCC Registration Number, photo ID, exam fee, directions or video-session link.'],
  ['Day 14', 'Exam day. One short quiz in the morning to warm up, nothing heavy. Arrive early, read every question to the end, and answer all 35.'],
];
---

<GuideLayout
  title={guide.title}
  description={guide.description}
  datePublished={guide.datePublished}
  eyebrow="HamCram guide"
  readingMinutes={guide.readingMinutes}
  ogImage="/images/hamcram-og.png"
  app={{
    name: 'HamCram',
    icon: '/images/hamcram-icon.png',
    href: '/hamcram',
    id: APP_IDS.hamCram,
    storeUrl,
    pitch:
      'Drills the current NCVEC Technician and General pools with Learn, flashcards, sized quizzes, and 35-question practice exams scored like test day, with weak-spot review and mastery by subelement.',
    price: '$9.99 once. No subscription, no ads, no account. Study data stays on your device.',
  }}
>
  <p>
    The Technician license is the entry ticket to amateur radio in the United States, and the
    exam that grants it is smaller than most people expect: 35 multiple-choice questions, 26
    correct to pass, drawn from a question pool that is published in full. Nothing on the test
    is a surprise. That makes two weeks of focused study a realistic plan for most adults who
    can give it 45 to 60 minutes a day. It is not a guarantee, and anyone who tells you
    otherwise is selling something; it is a plan that gives the material enough time to stick.
  </p>

  <h2>How the exam is built</h2>
  <p>
    Every question you can be asked comes from a public pool maintained by the National
    Conference of Volunteer Examiner Coordinators (NCVEC). The pool is replaced every four
    years, and the current Technician pool is valid through June 2030. It holds roughly 400
    questions, each with four answer choices and exactly one correct answer, and each question
    keeps its correct answer no matter which practice app or book you use.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pool is organized into ten <strong>subelements</strong> (T1 through T9, plus T0 for
    safety), and each subelement is divided into <strong>groups</strong>. There are 35 groups
    in total, and the exam takes exactly one question from each group. That single rule is the
    most useful thing to know before you study: a subelement with six groups puts six
    questions on your exam, a subelement with two groups puts two. Recent pools have used
    this split; confirm it against the printed pool you are studying.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Subelement</th><th>Topic</th><th>Questions on exam</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {subelements.map(([code, topic, n]) => (
          <tr><td>{code}</td><td>{topic}</td><td>{n}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Two things follow. First, you can miss nine questions and still pass, so a subelement you
    never fully understand is survivable if the rest is solid. Second, since each group
    contributes one question, time spent on a group with twelve pool questions is worth exactly
    as much on exam day as time spent on a group with four. Study by group, not by page count.
  </p>

  <h2>What to memorize and what to understand</h2>
  <p>
    Trying to understand everything is the slow way through this pool, and trying to memorize
    everything is the fragile way. Sort the material.
  </p>

  <h3>Memorize</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Rules and numbers.</strong> Station identification intervals, the frequency edges
      of the Technician privileges on the HF bands, power limits, what a control operator is,
      who may be a third party, how long a license lasts and how long the grace period runs.
      These have no logic to derive; they are definitions, and flashcards are the right tool.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Band and wavelength pairings.</strong> Know that the 2-meter band is 144 to 148
      MHz and the 70-centimeter band is 420 to 450 MHz the way you know your own phone number.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Q-signals, abbreviations, and phonetics.</strong> A handful appear in the pool.
      Learn the ones that do and ignore the rest for now.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The exact wording of safety answers.</strong> Safety questions are common sense,
      but the pool wants a specific answer among several reasonable-sounding ones.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Understand</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Ohm’s law and the power formula.</strong> Voltage equals current times
      resistance; power equals voltage times current. Practice rearranging them so a question
      that gives you power and voltage and asks for current does not feel like a new problem.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Frequency and wavelength.</strong> Wavelength in meters is approximately 300
      divided by frequency in MHz. Once that is in your head, the band names, antenna lengths,
      and several propagation questions stop being separate facts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Why VHF and UHF behave the way they do.</strong> Line-of-sight propagation,
      multipath, why a repeater on a hill matters, why you might hear a station on 10 meters
      from another continent but not on 2 meters.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What the blocks of a station do.</strong> Transceiver, power supply, feed line,
      antenna, SWR meter. Several questions ask what you would use to do something, and a
      mental picture answers all of them at once.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Modulation basics.</strong> What FM, SSB, and CW are for, and why each is used
      where it is. The digital-mode questions are mostly vocabulary on top of this.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>About the figures.</strong> The pool includes a few schematic diagrams. The
      questions ask you to identify a component in the figure; learning the handful of symbols
      involved takes ten minutes and is worth doing on Day 5.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The 14-day plan</h2>
  <p>
    Each day is 45 to 60 minutes. The plan front-loads the pure memorization (rules,
    procedures) because spaced repetition needs time to work, puts the conceptual material in
    the middle when you have the energy for it, and reserves the last four days for practice
    exams and weak spots. If you fall behind a day, drop a rest day rather than compressing two
    study days into one.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Day</th><th>What to do</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {plan.map(([day, what]) => (
          <tr><td>{day}</td><td>{what}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>How to use practice exams honestly</h2>
  <p>
    A practice exam is a measurement, not a study method. Taken too early, it only tells you
    that you have not studied yet; taken too often, you start to recognize questions by shape
    and your score drifts above what you actually know. Three to five full exams across the
    two weeks is plenty. Take them without notes and without pausing, and treat the misses as
    your reading list for the next day.
  </p>
  <p>
    Aim for consistent scores of 30 or better before exam day. The pass mark is 26, but a
    30-plus practice score leaves room for the nerves and the one question that is worded
    differently than you remember.
  </p>

  <h2>Logistics people forget</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>FCC Registration Number (FRN).</strong> You need one before the exam session, and
      you get it free from the FCC’s CORES system. Bring it with you.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Finding a session.</strong> Volunteer Examiner teams run sessions in person and
      by video. ARRL and the other VECs list upcoming sessions; many clubs test monthly.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fees.</strong> Expect a small session fee charged by the VE team and a separate
      FCC application fee after you pass. Check the current amounts; they change.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Photo ID.</strong> Required. If you plan to take the General exam on the same
      day after passing Technician, say so when you register.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where HamCram fits</h2>
  <p>
    Any practice tool that uses the real pool will work with this plan. HamCram is ours. It
    carries the complete Technician and General pools with every figure, a Learn mode that
    walks each subelement with the correct answers shown, flashcards for the memorization
    block, quizzes in 10, 25, 50, or 100 questions for the daily check, and full 35-question
    practice exams scored exactly like test day. Weak-spot review resurfaces the questions you
    keep missing, and mastery tracking shows your progress by subelement, which is what Day 11
    is built around. It costs $9.99 once, has no ads or account, and keeps your study data on
    the device.
  </p>
  <p>
    Whatever you use, the plan is the same: learn the rules early, understand the handful of
    ideas that generate most of the questions, measure yourself a few times, and show up with
    your FRN.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
